    "created_at": "2017-03-30T18:08:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-03-30T18:09:03Z",
    "body": "To be clear, WebKit and Blink do NOT follow the spec here in general.  What they do is actually _copy_ the marginwidth/marginheight attributes from the parent `<iframe>` to the body.  So this testcase:\r\n\r\n    <!DOCTYPE html>\r\n    <iframe marginwidth=\"100\"\r\n            srcdoc=\"<body><script>alert(document.body.attributes.length)</script></body>\">\r\n    </iframe>\r\n\r\nalerts 1 in Chrome and Safari, whereas per spec it should alert 0 (and does in Gecko and Edge).  They have a nice FIXME comment in the code for this.\r\n\r\nThen because they examine the body's marginwidth before the body's leftmargin, you get the behavior that the `<iframe>`'s marginwidth effectively takes precedence over leftmargin in WebKit/Blink.\r\n\r\nI just tested Edge 14, and it has the Gecko behavior: topmargin overrides parent iframe's marginwidth, leftmargin overrides marginheight.  Testcase:\r\n\r\n    <iframe marginwidth=\"10\" marginheight=\"10\"\r\n            src=\"data:text/html,<body topmargin=100 leftmargin=100>test\"></iframe>",
